---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Thinking of the AI - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="thinking-of-the-ai">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				The most useful mental model for working with AI tools: <strong class="text-stone-800">treat the AI like a junior employee who was hired five minutes ago and will leave the company as soon as the task is done.</strong>
			</p>

			<p>
				This framing does two important things. First, it correctly sets your expectations for the quality and type of output you'll get as well as how you should be framing your requests.
				We covered the actual competence level in the <a href="/intro/current-state">Current State of AI</a> section, where we compare it to an associate-level employee for strategy work and a new grad developer for development work.
			</p>

			<h3>They Know Nothing About Your Company & Jargon</h3>

			<p>
				A brand new employee doesn't know your project. They don't know the codebase, the architecture decisions, the client's preferences, the business rules that never got documented, or why that one endpoint is structured the way it is. They don't know your team's abbreviations, your Jira workflow, or what "the old system" refers to.
			</p>

			<p>
				AI tools are like new employees every single time. Even within a conversation where you've provided context, the AI has no background knowledge of your specific situation. It only knows what you tell it and what it can read from files you point it to.
			</p>

			<p>
				This means you need to get good at quickly bringing someone up to speed. The same skill that makes you effective at onboarding a new team member makes you effective at working with AI. This includes the quality of your documentation and the ability to quickly explain your project and requirements to the AI.
			</p>

			<p>
				Every company also develops its own shorthand. "Run it through the pipeline," "check the golden path," "update the BRD," "sync with the tiger team." These phrases are obvious to you and meaningless to a new hire. AI tools will either guess at what you mean (often wrong) or produce something generic that misses the point.
			</p>

			<h3>They Leave After the Task</h3>

			<p>
				Here's the part that trips people up: the AI doesn't carry context between conversations. That detailed explanation you gave it yesterday about your authentication flow? Gone. The corrections you made to its output last week? It doesn't remember them. Every new conversation starts from zero.
			</p>

			<p>
				You wouldn't rely on a temp worker's memory of last week's briefing. You'd give them the written instructions again. The same applies here.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is the case for sorting out requirements and documentation <em>before</em> you start, rather than discovering what you actually wanted halfway through. A clear brief up front gives the AI (and your teammates) something solid to work from and saves you from correcting the same misunderstanding over and over. We'll dig into this requirements-first, documentation-first way of working in a later chapter.
			</p>

			<p>
				It helps, but it isn't a panacea. Written context makes each fresh start faster and more accurate, but the AI is still starting fresh: it re-reads what you hand it with no memory of the reasoning behind it, and no judgment to notice when the brief itself is wrong. You still need to steer and check the output.
			</p>

			<h3>Document Your Project in the Project Itself</h3>

			<p>
				AI tools are incredibly fast readers; they will be able to re-read meeting notes, documents, code, anything very fast. If you keep a well-written set of documentation in the project itself, you can reference it in your requests to the AI and it will be able to find it quickly and easily.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">For development tools (Claude Code, Cursor, etc.)</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Most AI coding tools look for project-level instruction files: <code class="text-xs bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">AGENTS.md</code> (now the cross-tool standard, read by Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and others) or tool-specific files like <code class="text-xs bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">CLAUDE.md</code>. These files are automatically included in every conversation. Put your architecture overview, naming conventions, tech stack details, common commands, and team preferences here. It's the equivalent of a README that the AI actually reads every time. We cover this in depth in <a href="/task-breakdowns/agents-md">AGENTS.md</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">For strategy and general use</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Keep key context documents accessible and linkable. When you work in Gemini or Claude, you can attach relevant documents to the conversation. Having a well-maintained project brief, glossary, or decision log means you can attach it in seconds rather than re-explaining from scratch each time.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				This habit has a bonus effect: it improves your project for human team members too. If your documentation is good enough for an AI to produce useful output, it's probably good enough for a new hire to get up to speed quickly. The discipline of writing for the AI is really the discipline of writing clearly, and that benefits everyone.
			</p>

			<h3>Skills Can Help, But Aren't a Shortcut</h3>

			<p>
				One increasingly standard way to hand the AI reusable expertise is <a href="/requirements-writing/skills">skills</a>: small packaged sets of instructions and context that the AI pulls in on demand when a task matches. A skill can teach it your jargon, your house style, or the steps your team follows for a recurring task, so you don't have to re-explain them from scratch every time. We cover how to build and use them later in the guide.
			</p>

			<p>
				They help, but they don't change the underlying model. Even with good skills loaded, the AI is still the new employee: it applies the playbook you gave it without the judgment to know when the playbook doesn't fit. Skills raise the floor, they don't remove the need to check the work.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="The onboarding test">
				<p>
					Before you start a task with AI, ask yourself: "If a competent but completely new person sat down at my desk right now, would they have enough written context to do this task?" If the answer is no, the AI won't have enough context either. Write it down, put it in the project, and both humans and AI will benefit.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
